WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:04.080
The 2023 Oscars were filled&nbsp;
with plenty of highs and lows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:08.760
including a host who didn't exactly please&nbsp;
everyone. For the 95th Academy Awards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:14.280
Jimmy Kimmel was once again tapped as the emcee,&nbsp;
having previously hosted the ceremony in 2017 and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:20.130
2018. But the third time wasn't exactly the&nbsp;
charm, at least according to some viewers.

00:00:20.130 --> 00:00:23.940
Following Kimmel's opening monologue, which&nbsp;
highlighted the night's nominated films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:28.380
social media quickly began to blow up&nbsp;
with thoughts about his jokes. Multiple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:32.040
users thought his delivery timing was&nbsp;
off and that he maybe needed to slow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:36.569
down a bit. One person even referenced a&nbsp;
certain fictional conductor by tweeting,

00:00:36.569 --> 00:00:41.192
"We need Lydia Tár pushing Jimmy Kimmel&nbsp;
off the stage at the Oscars just for fun."

00:00:41.192 --> 00:00:46.020
While Kimmel's jokes weren't necessarily that&nbsp;
bad, it's always fun to see viewers come up with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:51.780
clever references to the nominated films. Kimmel&nbsp;
actually praised Tár, but some observers found his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:56.931
thoughts on the Cate Blanchett starring vehicle&nbsp;
to be condescending. As one person tweeted,

00:00:56.931 --> 00:01:00.360
"Jimmy Kimmel [is] making jokes&nbsp;
about Tár as if the entire scene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:04.365
he just mocked isn't a biting satire&nbsp;
on everything happening tonight."

00:01:04.365 --> 00:01:09.180
Others weren't exactly pleased when Kimmel went&nbsp;
negative. While the Oscars have never shied away&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:13.500
from calling out some of Hollywood's duds and&nbsp;
misfires, some viewers thought that his jab at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:19.020
the Damien Chazelle-directed Babylon went way&nbsp;
too far. As one person tweeted in all caps,

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:23.234
"GET JIMMY KIMMEL OFF THE STAGE I&nbsp;
DRAW THE LINE AT BABYLON SLANDER."

00:01:23.234 --> 00:01:26.660
Another fan of the Oscar-nominated&nbsp;
epic agreed by tweeting,

00:01:26.660 --> 00:01:30.360
"[I'm] gonna start the Jimmy Kimmel&nbsp;
hate train for the Babylon diss."

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:33.840
Some viewers even went so far as&nbsp;
to declare that Kimmel's monologue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:36.480
was a total bomb. As one of them bluntly tweeted,

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:39.720
"How many more years must we&nbsp;
be subjected to Jimmy Kimmel?"

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:44.520
Kimmel was also called out by Indian cinephiles&nbsp;
for referring to the Telugu-language film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:48.960
RRR as a Bollywood production, when&nbsp;
"Tollywood" would've been more accurate.

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:53.280
"Do you know Desi Naach?"

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:58.080
While Kimmel received his fair share of&nbsp;
flack on the internet, some other viewers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:02.580
were actually pleased with how the host was&nbsp;
mostly sharing the magic of cinema. He was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:07.380
praised for eliciting gasps and nervous laughter&nbsp;
from the crowd. At least one Twitter user went&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:12.151
so far as to declare the monologue one of the&nbsp;
best in recent memory. And as another noted,

00:02:12.151 --> 00:02:17.093
"Jimmy [Kimmel's] monologue [is] going well, good&nbsp;
job on the no women director [nomination] joke."

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:22.020
Kimmel was also positively compared to past&nbsp;
Oscar hosts like Bob Hope and Johnny Carson.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:26.100
And he enjoyed a callout to a spectacle&nbsp;
from across the pond, as someone tweeted,

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:31.360
"His parachute in was [a] ripoff of Queen and Bond&nbsp;
at [the] London Olympics [but] a better version."

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:36.120
Though Kimmel's third outing as Oscars host wasn't&nbsp;
able to please everyone, at least he was able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:41.815
find some fans on social media. But ultimately in&nbsp;
many ways, it doesn't really matter who the host&nbsp;is. 

00:02:41.815 --> 00:02:46.620
After all, viewers across the world are mainly&nbsp;
tuning in to celebrate cinema. And regarding that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:51.600
requirement, Kimmel did a decent job. His opening&nbsp;
monologue highlighted some of the year's most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:56.614
inventive films and reminded audiences just how&nbsp;
important seeing movies on the big screen is.

00:02:56.614 --> 00:03:01.560
Kimmel even went ahead and casually called out&nbsp;
James Cameron and Tom Cruise for not showing up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:06.180
to the ceremony, even though they're two of the&nbsp;
theatrical experience's most ardent supporters.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:10.020
Some Twitter users although that Kimmel&nbsp;
deserves praise for not shying away from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:13.162
pointing out how no women were&nbsp;
nominated for Best Director this&nbsp;year. 

00:03:13.162 --> 00:03:16.320
And they also found it hard not&nbsp;
to appreciate him for dragging Warner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:20.701
Bros. Discovery over their cancellation of&nbsp;
the Batgirl movie starring Leslie Grace.

00:03:20.701 --> 00:03:25.260
Kimmel wasn't perfect, but he also doesn't&nbsp;
deserve a toxic spew of hatred. Then again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:28.675
the jokes pitting him against&nbsp;
Lydia Tár were pretty hilarious.

00:03:29.103 --> 00:03:32.823
"Who, may I ask, gets to decide that?"

00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:37.080
This year, more than ever, it's&nbsp;
crystal clear that the Oscars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:40.740
wanted to celebrate the movies. And Jimmy&nbsp;
Kimmel clearly understood the assignment.

